    val starlitSea: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "The Keeper" to """
            The ferry stopped coming to Varal in the third year of the new timetable, and nobody on the mainland noticed, because nobody on the mainland had ever been. Noor noticed. She noticed the way you notice a clock that has stopped: not immediately, but as a slow wrongness in the day, a silence where a sound used to sit.

            The lighthouse did not need her. It had not needed anyone since the automation crews came through a decade earlier and left behind a grey box that hummed and a laminated card that said, in four languages, DO NOT ADJUST. Noor had not adjusted it. She had, however, kept the brass polished, the stairs swept, and the logbook filled, because her father had done those things and his mother before him, and because an island is only an island if someone is counting the days on it.

            Every evening at nine she climbed the hundred and twelve steps to the lamp room, where the old radio sat beneath a square of oilcloth. It was a marine set, heavy as a stone, with a dial that glowed the colour of weak tea. She would switch it on, let it warm, and turn the dial slowly from one end of the band to the other, listening to the sea's own static. Then she would say, into the microphone, the same thing she had said for eleven years: "Varal light, Varal light. Anyone on this frequency, come back."

            Nobody came back. She had stopped expecting it long ago. The saying of it had become something else — a bell rung in an empty church, a way of telling the dark that the island was still here, still lit, still counting.

            She kept the log anyway. 21:04. Clear. Wind NW, light. Sea calm. No traffic. And underneath, in the column her grandmother had added and nobody had ever removed: Stars — all present.

            That was the night the frequency answered.
        """,
        "The Frequency" to """
            It began as a tone, so faint she thought it was the set itself, a capacitor somewhere deciding to fail. Then the tone broke into rhythm, and the rhythm into something that was almost a voice, the way a face is almost a face in the grain of a door.

            "— light," it said. "Varal light. Reading you."

            Noor did not move for a long time. The oilcloth was still bunched in her left hand. Outside, the beam went round once, twice, laying its white stripe across the water and lifting it away again. When she finally pressed the key, her voice came out as her father's: level, formal, a voice for the sea.

            "Station calling Varal light, say again your identity and position."

            A pause, long enough that she began to compose the explanation she would give herself in the morning. Then: "No position. Not one you'd find. But I've been listening a long time. I liked that you said the stars were all present."

            She had never said that aloud. It was a thing she wrote. She looked at the logbook as though it might be transmitting on its own.

            "Who is this?"

            "Someone who kept a light once," the voice said, and there was a smile in it, she was sure, the particular smile of a person who has been alone so long that company feels like a joke only they understand. "You're doing it properly. The sweep, the log, the call. Most of them stopped years ago. The boxes do it now."

            "The box does the lamp," Noor said. "It doesn't do the rest."

            "No," the voice agreed. "It doesn't."

            They talked until the dial had gone cold again and the first grey had come into the east. Noor did not write any of it down. In the log, under 21:04, she added a second line, and then crossed it out, and then wrote it again, smaller: Traffic — one station. Unidentified. Friendly.
        """,
        "Stars, All Present" to """
            The voice came back on the nights the sea was flat, which on Varal was one night in four. It never gave a name. It knew the island — the cistern behind the cottage, the place where the path had slipped in her grandmother's time, the shape of the rock the gulls preferred — but it knew the island as it had been, not as it was. When Noor mentioned the solar array on the south slope, there was a silence that she learned to recognise as the voice adjusting to news.

            She stopped asking who it was. The question had an answer, and she found she could live beside the answer without opening it, the way you can live beside a locked room.

            Instead she told it things. The ferry timetable. The year the puffins had not come. The way her father had sat on the top step in his last month, not looking at the sea but at the stairs, as if counting them for a report. The voice listened the way the island listened: completely, without interrupting, with the sense that the listening itself was a kind of weather.

            One night in late October it said, "You should put the ferry back."

            "I can't put the ferry back. It's a company. In an office. On the mainland."

            "You put the light back," the voice said. "Every night. You put the stars back. You write them down and there they are. Someone has to be the one who insists."

            Noor laughed, and the laugh surprised her, because it was not bitter. She looked at the log, at eleven years of her handwriting insisting, night after night, that the island was here, the lamp was lit, the stars were all present. She thought of the grey box humming beneath her, doing the lamp, not doing the rest.

            In the morning she walked down to the slipway, where the old ferry notice still hung in its rusted frame, and with a marker from the kitchen drawer she wrote across the blank timetable: VARAL. LIGHT KEPT. VISITORS WELCOME. ENQUIRE ON CHANNEL 16.

            It was, she admitted, not a ferry. But it was a signal. And someone, somewhere, might be listening.

            That night the sea was flat. She climbed the hundred and twelve steps, switched on the set, and waited for the dial to glow. "Varal light, Varal light," she said. "Anyone on this frequency, come back."

            And for the first time in eleven years, she meant it as a question.
        """,
    )

    val neonHarbor: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "Warm" to """
            The complaint came in at 11:52 and it was the wrong kind of complaint. Ira Bose had taken a thousand calls from the docks — noise, theft, a crane operator who believed in ghosts — and she had never once been told that a cold-storage unit was warm.

            "Warm how?" she said.

            "Warm like a hand," the caller said. He gave a name, a berth number, and a reason for being on the quay at midnight that she did not believe and did not write down. Then the line went to a tone that was not quite a dial tone, and when she rang back the number did not exist.

            Neon Harbor at midnight is a city that has taken off its face. The signs are still lit — fish, noodles, girls, loans — but the people who meant them have gone home, and what is left is light with no intention behind it. Ira drove the length of the quay with her window down, letting the rain come in. The rain here tasted of diesel and oranges; there was a juice plant upriver that nobody had ever found.

            Unit 14 sat at the end of the row, a steel box the size of a chapel, its compressor silent. She put her palm against the door. The caller had been exact. Warm like a hand. Not hot. Not the heat of a fire or a fault. The warmth of something that had been alive, recently and at length, on the other side of an inch of steel.

            She did not open it. She had been a detective long enough to know that a door you open at midnight is a door you own by morning. Instead she photographed the seal, noted the number of the padlock, and walked back along the quay to her car, where the radio was saying her name.

            Dispatch wanted to know why she was at the docks. There was no record of a complaint.
        """,
        "The Man Who Wasn't" to """
            The name the caller had given belonged to a shipping clerk called Benedict Mallo, who had worked for Harbour Consolidated for nineteen years and who had, according to Harbour Consolidated, never existed. Ira sat in their lobby at nine the next morning with a coffee she had not been offered and listened to a woman in a grey suit explain, with great patience, that there was no Mallo in the system, had never been a Mallo, and that she was welcome to search the system herself, which she did, and there wasn't.

            There was, however, a desk. It was on the fourth floor by a window that looked out at Unit 14, and it had been cleared the way a desk is cleared by someone in a hurry who wants it to look like someone leaving slowly. The drawers were empty. The chair had been adjusted for someone short. Taped to the underside of the keyboard tray, where a man who did not exist might hide something from people who insisted on it, was a key.

            It was a padlock key. She knew the number before she read it.

            The woman in the grey suit walked her to the lift. "Detective," she said, as the doors closed, "you understand we're a very old company." It was not a threat. It was something worse: an apology, in advance, for things the company was about to do.

            Ira spent the afternoon doing what she always did when a case began to feel like a corridor with the lights going out behind her: she went to the records office and read. Harbour Consolidated had owned Unit 14 since 1961. Before that it had belonged to a firm called Cordwainer & Lusk, which had gone bankrupt in a single week after its principal, one Augustus Lusk, had been found in his own cold store, perfectly preserved, having apparently locked himself in. There had been a daughter. The daughter had married a Mallo.

            Outside, the rain had stopped, and the neon had come on early, and all of it was lying.
        """,
        "Channel 16" to """
            She opened the door at 2 a.m. because that is when a city stops pretending, and because she had decided, somewhere between the records office and the third coffee, that she would rather own it than wonder.

            The key turned the way keys turn in films: too easily, as if the lock had been waiting. Inside, Unit 14 was not a cold store. It had not been a cold store for a long time. The racks had been pulled out and replaced with desks, and on the desks were radios — marine sets, dozens of them, old and heavy, their dials glowing the colour of weak tea — and every one of them was on.

            The warmth was the radios. Fifty valve sets, running for days, had turned a chapel of steel into a slow oven. Ira stood in the doorway and listened to the sea's own static coming from fifty directions at once, and under the static, voices. Lighthouse keepers. Weather stations. A woman on an island somewhere saying, "Varal light, Varal light, anyone on this frequency, come back."

            Benedict Mallo was in the last chair, with his back to her and headphones on. He was short. He was also, she saw as she came around the desk, very much a man who existed, and had been existing in this room for approximately four days without food, and had reached the far side of that experience into a kind of calm.

            "They said you'd come," he said, without taking the headphones off. "The company. They said someone always comes. I thought it would be them."

            "Who are you listening to?"

            "Everyone they stopped listening to." He gestured at the sets, and the gesture took in the whole harbour, the whole lit and lying city. "When they automated the lights, they turned off the watch. All these people still calling. Nobody logging it. Lusk started this. My great-grandfather. He kept the watch here after they took it off him. They found him in his chair."

            Ira looked at the padlock in her hand, and at the woman in the grey suit's apology, and at the fifty glowing dials. There were, she calculated, perhaps forty minutes before the company sent whoever it sent.

            "Mr Mallo," she said, "I'm going to need you to teach me how to use one of these. Quickly."

            He took the headphones off, then, and looked at her for the first time, and the look was the look of a man who has been alone so long that company feels like a joke only he understands.

            "Channel sixteen," he said. "Start there. Someone's always there."
        """,
    )

    val clockworkForest: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "The Slowing" to """
            Pell knew the forest was slowing before anyone told her, because the owls were late.

            In the Clockwork Forest the owls do not hoot; they tick, and they tick in time, and the time they keep is the forest's own. For as long as anyone could remember — which in the forest was a very long time, since the trees kept the years in their rings and would show them to you if you asked politely — the owls had ticked at exactly sixty to the minute. Now they ticked at fifty-eight. Then fifty-six. Pell, who was a tuner's apprentice and had an ear for such things, lay awake counting and feeling the forest's great heart drag.

            "It's the Spring," said old Marrow, the master tuner, when she told him. He was oiling the hinge of a fern, which had begun to creak in the mornings. "The Great Spring at the centre. It winds down, once in a long age, and someone has to go and wind it up."

            "Who?"

            Marrow looked at his hands, which were eighty years old and shook in the cold, and then at hers, which were sixteen and did not. He did not need to say anything.

            She left at dawn with a tuning fork, a pot of oil, and a key the size of her forearm that had hung above the workshop door since before the door. The key was iron and very plain, and it was heavier than it had any right to be, as if it knew what it was for.
        """,
        "The Chiming River" to """
            The path to the centre was not on any map, because the forest rearranged its paths every night the way a sleeper rearranges blankets. Marrow had given her the only guidance there was: "Follow the slowing. Where the ticking is slowest, that's the way in."

            So she walked with her head cocked, listening, and the forest unfolded around her in brass and green. The leaves of the beech trees were thin copper plates that turned in the wind with a sound like coins; the moss was velvet laid over gears; the squirrels ran on tiny ratchets and paused, when they saw her, with an audible click. Everything was beautiful and everything was late.

            On the second day she came to the river. It chimed. The water fell over a weir of struck bronze and every drop rang a note, so that the river was a long cold song moving east. Pell sat on the bank and listened for the pattern, because Marrow had taught her that everything in the forest had a pattern and the pattern was always the way in.

            The song was slowing too. Notes that should have been quavers were crotchets. The river was running on the forest's heart, and the heart was tired.

            She crossed on a bridge of stopped pendulums, each one a step, each one hanging dead still at the bottom of its arc. It occurred to her halfway across that if the Spring ran down completely, she would be standing on a bridge of pendulums in a forest of stopped owls with a key she had not yet used, and that nobody would come, because nobody could, because coming required time and time required the Spring.

            She walked faster.
        """,
        "The Great Spring" to """
            The centre of the forest was a clearing, and in the clearing was a tree, and the tree was hollow, and inside the hollow was the Spring.

            It was not what she had expected. She had imagined something vast — a coil the size of a house, a mechanism to make Marrow weep. Instead there was a brass drum no bigger than a barrel, set into the heartwood, with a keyhole in its face and a slow, slow turning inside it, like breath drawn by someone very old and very far away.

            There was also a chair. And in the chair, a tuner. She had been there a long time; her coat had moss on it and the moss had gears in it and the gears were turning, very slowly, in time with the drum. She was asleep, or something past asleep. Her hands rested on a key exactly like Pell's.

            Pell understood then what Marrow had not said: that the Spring was not wound once in a long age. It was wound by someone who stayed. Who sat in the chair, and listened to the forest's heart, and turned the key a quarter-turn every day for as long as there were days, until their hands shook in the cold and someone younger came up the path with the slowing in her ears.

            She set down the oil and the fork. She fitted the key. It went in the way keys go in when the lock has been waiting.

            The first turn was the hardest; the Spring had settled into its tiredness and did not want to be reminded. The second was easier. On the third, far away through the hollow trees, she heard an owl tick — late, and then less late, and then exactly on the minute, and then another, and another, until the whole forest was ticking in time again and the river had gone back to its quavers.

            The old tuner opened her eyes. They were very bright. "Oh, good," she said, as if Pell were a little late for tea. "I was starting to lose count."

            Pell pulled up the second chair. There was, she had noticed, always a second chair.
        """,
    )

    val crimsonDune: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "The Cartographer Lies" to """
            The cartographer's name was Aurel and he lied the way other men breathed: constantly, and without apparent effort, and only occasionally about anything that mattered. Tariq had guided eleven caravans across the Red Waste and he had never before been handed a map by a man who then said, pleasantly, "Of course, half of this is invented."

            "Which half?"

            Aurel smiled. He had a smile that had been expensive to acquire. "If I knew that, I wouldn't need you."

            The caravan was small — nine camels, four men, a woman called Sefa who did not speak and whose job nobody explained — and it left the last well at Qem on the morning of the first cold wind. Tariq rode at the front, as he always did, reading the sand. The Waste did not have landmarks; it had habits. A dune that leaned east would lean east tomorrow; a hollow that held shade at noon would hold it in a month. You did not navigate the Waste. You remembered it.

            On the third night, Aurel unrolled the map by the fire and pointed to a feature he had drawn in red ink, a long crescent dune with a mark beneath it like a closed eye. "That one's real," he said. "I'm almost sure."

            "Almost."

            "Well." Aurel rolled the map. "It moved."
        """,
        "Against the Wind" to """
            Dunes move. Everyone who has crossed the Waste knows it: the wind takes the crest grain by grain and lays it down on the lee side, and the whole hill walks, a few paces a year, in the direction the wind prefers. The wind over the Waste blew from the north. The dunes walked south. This was as reliable as thirst.

            The Crimson Dune walked north.

            Tariq found its track on the fifth day — a long low ridge of disturbed sand, running against the wind like a scar — and he sat his camel for a long time looking at it before he said anything. Then he said: "Tell me what's under it."

            Aurel had the grace not to lie immediately. He looked at Sefa, who looked at the ridge, and something passed between them that had nothing to do with maps. "A door," Aurel said finally. "Or a thing that was a door. I found it in a book that was itself mostly invented. The dune covers it, and uncovers it, and covers it again, and it does this against the wind, because the thing under the door doesn't want to be found and doesn't want to be lost either. It wants to be looked for."

            "And you want to look."

            "I want," Aurel said, "to draw it. Properly. Once. Before I die." It was, Tariq thought, the first true thing he had said since Qem.

            They followed the scar for two more days, north, into the oldest part of the Waste, where the sand was the colour of dried blood and the stars came down close enough to touch. Sefa began, on the second night, to hum. The camels did not like it. Tariq found that he did.
        """,
        "What the Map Forgot" to """
            They reached the dune at the hour before dawn, when the Waste is briefly blue. It stood alone on the plain, a crescent half a mile long, its crest sharp as a drawn line, and at its foot, where the sand had walked away from it in the night, was the door.

            It was not a door. It was a frame — two uprights and a lintel of black stone, with nothing between them but more desert — and it was older than the dune, older than the Waste, older, Tariq felt with a certainty that had no business in a practical man, than the idea of doors. The sand around it was perfectly smooth. There were no footprints but theirs.

            Aurel got down from his camel with his drawing case and his hands were shaking, and Tariq understood, finally, why a man who lied for a living had come all this way to draw something true.

            Sefa walked to the frame and stood in it. Nothing happened. Then she turned, and looked back at them through the empty stone, and Tariq saw that on her side of the lintel the stars were different.

            "Draw quickly," she said. It was the first time she had spoken. Her voice was the voice of the humming. "It covers again at sunrise. It always covers again."

            Aurel drew. Tariq watched the sky go from blue to grey to the first thread of gold, and watched the dune begin, grain by grain, to walk — north, against the wind, back over the frame, back over the woman standing in it, who did not move, who was still looking at him when the sand reached her knees, her waist, her face, and then there was only a crescent of red sand on a blue plain, and a cartographer sitting with a finished drawing in his lap and tears running into his beard.

            "Is it true?" Tariq said. "The map. Now."

            Aurel looked at it for a long time. Then he took his pen, and in the margin beside the closed eye, in his small expensive hand, he wrote: Sefa. And beneath it: Here. And then, after a moment, because he could not help himself: Probably.
        """,
    )

    val winterGarden: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "Frost, Cultivated" to """
            The first thing Odile learned at the Winter Garden was that nothing in it was allowed to grow.

            "Growth is for gardeners," said Master Verenne, walking her along the frozen beds on her first morning. "We are not gardeners. We are alchemists who happen to keep a garden. Our business is not growth. It is change."

            The beds were laid out like any walled garden — herbs by the kitchen, fruit against the south wall, a long border for cutting — but everything in them was frost. Not frosted: made of frost. Ferns of it, grown an inch a night on wires strung between stakes. Roses of it, pale and exact, that Verenne pruned with warmed shears so that they would not shatter. Along the south wall, trained against the brick, a frost vine bore fruit the size of plums, each one a clear globe of ice with something turning slowly at its centre.

            "What's inside them?" Odile asked.

            "Patience," Verenne said, and it was a week before Odile understood that this was not a rebuke but an answer.

            The work was slow and cold and precise. She learned to breathe away from the beds. She learned which hours of the night the frost grew fastest, and which winds killed it, and how to coax a crystal along a wire with the warmth of a single fingertip held an inch away. She learned that a thing can be made to change by being kept perfectly, exactly, unbearably still.

            She did not learn what Verenne was making. The master worked alone, in the glasshouse at the garden's centre, and the glass was frosted from within.
        """,
        "The Glasshouse" to """
            Odile had been at the garden a year when the fruit on the south wall began to ripen.

            It did not soften, or colour. It cleared. The ice of each globe went from cloudy to glass to something clearer than glass, and the thing turning at its centre became visible: a seed. An ordinary seed, brown and small, of a kind she did not know. It turned slowly, as if in water, as if considering.

            Verenne spent longer and longer in the glasshouse. The master came out grey-faced at dawn and ate standing up and went back. One morning she did not come out at all, and Odile — who had been told, firmly and often, that the glasshouse was not hers — stood at its door for the length of time it takes a frost fern to grow a quarter-inch, and then opened it.

            Inside, the glasshouse was warm. That was the first shock. The second was the bed in the centre, which was not frost but earth, black and damp and breathing, the only living soil in the whole garden. And in the bed, grown to the height of Odile's shoulder, a tree.

            It was not a frost tree. It was wood, and leaf, and under the leaves, fruit — real fruit, dull gold, heavy on the branch. Verenne sat on a stool beside it with her hands in her lap, and her hands, Odile saw, were the colour of the bark.

            "You were turning lead," Odile said. It was what apprentices were told. It was what the books said alchemists did.

            "Nobody turns lead," Verenne said. Her voice had gone quiet and rough. "Lead is happy as lead. I was turning winter. One seed at a time. Every fruit on that wall is a winter I grew still enough to change." She lifted one bark-coloured hand toward the tree. "This is where they go when they're done."

            "Into what?"

            "Into a spring," said the master, "that someone else will have to tend."
        """,
        "Something Other Than Lead" to """
            Verenne did not die so much as finish. Odile sat with her through the last night, in the warm glasshouse with the gold fruit shining, and toward dawn the master's breathing slowed, and slowed, and became the slow turning of a seed in ice, and then stopped, and the stool was empty, and the bed of black earth had one more tree in it.

            Odile did not weep immediately. She was an alchemist's apprentice; she had been taught that change deserved attention before grief. She walked the two trees, and touched the bark of the new one, which was warm, and then she went out into the frozen garden and stood in the blue cold and let herself understand what she had inherited.

            A garden where nothing grew. A wall of winters, ripening. A warm room at the centre where, one at a time, with patience, the winters were turned into something else.

            It would take her life. That was clear. It had taken Verenne's, and whoever had sat on the stool before Verenne, and whoever before that; the glasshouse was full of trees, now that she looked, each a little older, each the colour of a different pair of hands. It was not a thing one did and then left. It was a thing one became.

            She went back to the south wall, where the frost vine hung heavy with clear fruit, and she chose one — the smallest, the one with the seed that turned a little faster than the rest — and she warmed her shears, and she cut it down.

            It was heavier than it looked. Everything in the garden was.

            In the glasshouse she knelt by the black bed and pressed the globe of ice into the earth, and breathed on it, once, the way Verenne had taught her never to breathe on the beds, and watched the frost begin, very slowly, to change its mind.
        """,
    )

    val coffeeRain: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "The Power Goes Out" to """
            The storm took the lights at 7:40, which Nila knew because she had just looked at the clock and thought, twenty minutes and I can close, and then the clock was gone along with everything else and Café Monsoon was a dark room full of rain-sound and two people breathing.

            One of them was her. The other was sitting at the window table, where he had been sitting for forty minutes with a coffee he had not drunk, and his name was Teo, and twelve years ago he had left this town on a Tuesday without saying a word to her, and she had spent a reasonable number of those years deciding what she would say to him if he ever walked back in, and now he had, and the lights had gone, and what she said was: "Don't move. There's a step."

            "I remember the step," he said.

            She found the candles by feel — they were where they had always been, in the drawer under the register, because her mother had put them there and Nila had never seen a reason to move them — and lit three, and set one on his table, and one on the counter, and held the third for a moment without knowing where to put it.

            "You cut your hair," he said.

            "Twelve years ago."

            "Right." He looked at the coffee. "I should have — "

            "Yes." She put the candle down. "You should have."
        """,
        "Twelve Years" to """
            The thing about a power cut is that it makes a room honest. There is nothing to look at but the other person and nothing to do but talk, and the rain does the work of making the silence bearable.

            He told her where he had been. It was not interesting — a city, a job, another city, a different job, a marriage that had ended the way weather ends, without anyone being wrong — and she realised halfway through that she had expected it to be interesting, had needed it to be, because an uninteresting reason for leaving was so much worse than a dramatic one.

            "I thought you'd be gone," he said. "I came back for my father's house and I thought, she'll be in Delhi, or London, or somewhere with a river, and I'll walk past the café and it'll be a phone shop. And it isn't. And you're not."

            "No," said Nila. "I'm not."

            "Why?"

            She considered lying. It would have been easy; she was good at it, she had had practice, she had told a hundred customers that she stayed because she loved the town. But the candle was between them, and the rain was doing its work, and she was tired.

            "Because someone had to be the one who stayed," she said. "That's all. You all left — you, and Priya, and the Mehta boys — and the café was here, and my mother was here, and I thought, if I leave too, then there's no here. There's just a place people used to be from." She picked up his cold coffee and took it away and started making another, in the dark, by feel. "And then it was ten years. And then it was twelve."

            "Nila."

            "Don't. I'm not sad. I'm telling you because you asked and because the lights are out." She set the fresh cup in front of him. "Drink it this time."
        """,
        "Second Chances" to """
            The rain eased around nine. Neither of them mentioned it.

            Teo drank the coffee. He talked about his father's house, which had a roof that needed doing and a garden that had gone to jungle, and she heard under the talk the thing he was not asking, which was whether he should stay long enough to do the roof. She did not answer it. She was not sure she had an answer. Twelve years is a long time to be the one who stayed, and it leaves a shape in you, and the shape does not simply open because the person it was made around has walked back in with his hair going grey at the sides.

            But.

            She had noticed the but around the second candle. It was small. It was the way he had said I remember the step. It was the fact that he had sat for forty minutes with a coffee he did not drink, in a café he could have walked past, in a town he had every reason to think had forgotten him, waiting for her to come out from the back. It was that he had come back for a house, and stayed for the rain.

            "The roof," she said, when he had run out of things to say about it. "Who's doing it?"

            "I don't know yet. I'd have to find someone."

            "Ravi Mehta does roofs now. The younger one. He came back three years ago." She paused. "People do, sometimes."

            Teo looked at her across the candle, and she let him look, and did not look away, and that was the whole of it, really: twelve years, and a storm, and two people in a dark room not saying the thing, and then one of them not looking away.

            The lights came back at 9:14. The fridge shuddered awake; the clock blinked twelve. The room was suddenly ordinary, and bright, and full of the small ugly details candles are kind enough to hide, and Nila felt the shape in her tighten, as it always did, as it had for twelve years.

            Then Teo reached across and blew out the candle on his table, and the one on the counter, and said, "I liked it better before."

            And she laughed, and it was not bitter, and she went and turned the lights off again.
        """,
    )

    val unfinishedChronicles: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "Openings" to """
            I keep a drawer of first lines. Most writers do, I think, though we call it different things: a notebook, a file, a folder on the desktop named IDEAS that we open rarely and with a feeling close to dread. Mine is an actual drawer, the top left of a desk I bought in a town I no longer live in, and it is full of index cards, and every card has one sentence on it, and not one of those sentences has a second.

            Here is one: The ferry stopped coming to Varal in the third year of the new timetable, and nobody noticed. I wrote that eight years ago. I know exactly who Varal is for and what happens at the lighthouse and why the radio matters, and I have never written the second sentence, and I am no longer sure I ever will.

            Here is another: Every light in the city is lying to someone. I wrote that on a train. I do not know what city. I do not know what the lie is. I like it anyway. I like it more, honestly, than most of the stories I have finished, because a finished story has to choose, and a first line gets to be every story at once.

            This essay — this collection of essays — is an attempt to be honest about the drawer. Not to empty it; I have tried that, and the cards come back. But to open it in company, and look at what is in there, and ask the question I have been avoiding for most of my working life, which is: what is the difference between a story I have not written yet and a story I am not going to write? And is the second kind a failure, or is it something else?
        """,
        "The Ones Who Wandered Off" to """
            Characters leave. This is not a metaphor. You are writing along, and a person you have invented walks out of the scene for a perfectly good reason — to fetch something, to answer a door — and does not come back. You wait. You write around the gap. Eventually you realise the gap is the story now, and the person is somewhere else, in a story you cannot see, having a better time.

            I had a cartographer once. He lied beautifully. I built him a desert and a caravan and a dune that moved the wrong way, and on the fifth day of the crossing he unrolled his map by the fire and said something to the guide that I had not planned, and the guide said something back, and I understood that the two of them had a history I did not know about and were not going to tell me.

            I put the pages away. That was six years ago. The cartographer is still out there, somewhere in the red sand, drawing something true. I think about him more than I think about most people I have actually met.

            For a long time I treated this as a problem to be solved — outline harder, know your characters, do the exercises where you write their childhoods. And the exercises work, in the sense that you end up with characters who do what they are told. But the ones who do what they are told are not the ones I remember. The ones I remember wandered off. And I have started to wonder whether the wandering is the point: whether the stories that stay unfinished are the ones where I made someone real enough to have somewhere else to be.
        """,
        "Half-Told" to """
            My grandmother told stories the way some people hum: constantly, half-attending, never quite finishing. A story about a goat would become a story about a flood would become a story about her sister, and then the kettle would boil, and that was that. As a child I found this maddening. I wanted endings. I wanted to know what happened to the goat.

            She died when I was nineteen, and for a long time afterward I tried to finish her stories, writing them down the way I thought they should have gone. They were terrible. They were terrible in a specific way: they were complete, and completeness was exactly the thing her stories had never had and never needed. The goat was not the point. The sister was not the point. The point was a woman at a kitchen table with her hands around a cup, going on, and the kettle, and the going on.

            I think this is what the drawer is for. Not a graveyard. A kitchen table.

            Some stories are better left half-told. Not because the teller failed, but because a half-told story is a door left open, and the reader walks through it, and the story goes on in a room the writer cannot see. The lighthouse keeper is still calling on channel 16. The cartographer is still drawing. The café is dark and the rain has not stopped and two people are still not saying the thing. They are not finished. They are not going to be. They are, I have decided, fine.

            I am going to close the drawer now. I will open it again; I always do. But I wanted, once, to leave it open in company, and show you what was inside, and say: these are mine, and they are not done, and that is not the same as nothing.

            The kettle is boiling. That is that.
        """,
    )
